<div class="oc-video-container">
<p><a href="https://www.openculture.com/?post_type=post&amp;p=1127540"><img decoding="async" src="https://i.ytimg.com/vi/r61swUZHUP4/maxresdefault.jpg" width="800" border="0"></a></p>
</div>
<p>	<!-- /oc-video-embed -->
</p></div>
<p><!-- /oc-video-wrapper --></p>
<p>It must be said that some of the blame lies with Murray himself, whose goofball instincts clash against the nineteen-twenties setting; as he later admitted, he and director John Byrum were wrong to insist on a period piece. (Just imagine the possibilities of Murray playing a returned Vietnam veteran instead.) Regardless, he continued to follow his inner Larry in the aftermath, decamping to Paris with his young family in order to live and learn far from the American scene he knew. It was there that he encountered the teachings of the mystic G. I. Gurdjieff, whose influence on Murray’s persona <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2016/06/the-philosophy-of-bill-murray-the-intellectual-foundations-of-his-comedic-persona.html">we’ve previously featured here on Open Culture</a>. That marked another step along the path of experience that would lead him to play wiser, sadder, yet never entirely unfunny characters in pictures like Wes Anderson’s&nbsp;<a href="https://www.openculture.com/2018/12/wes-andersons-breakthrough-film-rushmore-revisited-in-five-video-essays.html"><em>Rushmore</em></a> and Sofia Coppola’s&nbsp;<em>Lost in Translation </em>— and, in so doing, win dramatic respectability after all.</p>
